00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:19.100
In 2008, a small startup
called Waze was formed.

00:01:19.100 --> 00:01:21.920
We hit the ground
running with a mission

00:01:21.920 --> 00:01:27.020
to help our users
outsmart traffic together.

00:01:27.020 --> 00:01:30.020
To achieve that mission, we
needed very fast feedback

00:01:30.020 --> 00:01:32.070
from our users.

00:01:32.070 --> 00:01:33.860
How did we get it?

00:01:33.860 --> 00:01:37.780
We ship software features,
and we ship them fast.

00:01:37.780 --> 00:01:41.530
We did that using a few
developers deploying

00:01:41.530 --> 00:01:43.810
to a few physical servers.

00:01:43.810 --> 00:01:48.360
However, we couldn't scale
on physical hardware.

00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:53.540
So then we moved to the cloud.

00:01:53.540 --> 00:01:56.030
Once we were in the
cloud, things really

00:01:56.030 --> 00:01:58.130
started getting complicated.

00:01:58.130 --> 00:02:01.550
Till now, the developers
were able to deploy software

00:02:01.550 --> 00:02:04.320
to production on their own.

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:07.220
But as the amount
of users grew, we

00:02:07.220 --> 00:02:10.800
had to start using
autoscaling massively,

00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:14.690
until we ended up with
hundreds of autoscaling groups

00:02:14.690 --> 00:02:17.980
and thousands of instances.

00:02:17.980 --> 00:02:20.260
The more we grew,
the more severe

00:02:20.260 --> 00:02:23.350
the problem became,
until eventually,

00:02:23.350 --> 00:02:27.400
the entire development
team lost this capability

00:02:27.400 --> 00:02:29.620
to deliver software
to production.

00:02:29.620 --> 00:02:32.320
It simply evaporated.

00:02:32.320 --> 00:02:35.810
And that was mainly
due to lack of tooling.

00:02:35.810 --> 00:02:40.220
Back then, to deploy one
backend service to production,

00:02:40.220 --> 00:02:43.520
developers needed
to create images,

00:02:43.520 --> 00:02:46.700
deploy them to
multiple geographically

00:02:46.700 --> 00:02:51.860
charted autoscaling groups,
usually in a rolling fashion.

00:02:51.860 --> 00:02:55.570
And rollbacks were
extremely painful.

00:02:55.570 --> 00:02:59.890
So this job moved
to the SRE team,

00:02:59.890 --> 00:03:03.040
which is in charge
of Waze production.

00:03:03.040 --> 00:03:07.160
But at least we
were able to grow.

00:03:07.160 --> 00:03:13.140
So we started out with a goal
of fast feedback from our users.

00:03:13.140 --> 00:03:17.920
But as we hit a certain scale
of Waze users, developers,

00:03:17.920 --> 00:03:20.070
and microservices,
the complexity

00:03:20.070 --> 00:03:25.620
of software deployment
went up dramatically.

00:03:25.620 --> 00:03:29.250
So things were kind
of grinding to a halt.

00:03:29.250 --> 00:03:31.580
There was this bottleneck,
but nobody really

00:03:31.580 --> 00:03:35.740
realized just how bad it was.

00:03:35.740 --> 00:03:38.830
Once developers couldn't ship
as fast as they wanted to,

00:03:38.830 --> 00:03:43.060
they started batching features
into the same release.

00:03:43.060 --> 00:03:46.720
And it became riskier to
deploy like that to production.

00:03:46.720 --> 00:03:49.110
Even though they
wanted to ship fast

00:03:49.110 --> 00:03:53.050
once features were completed,
they simply could not.

00:03:53.050 --> 00:03:56.290
And because of the batching
of features and bug fixes,

00:03:56.290 --> 00:04:00.560
releases became extremely large.

00:04:00.560 --> 00:04:03.140
And it's nasty to
work like this,

00:04:03.140 --> 00:04:06.620
because by now,
you are deploying

00:04:06.620 --> 00:04:10.490
very large pieces of software
that could break the system.

00:04:10.490 --> 00:04:12.890
And when it breaks,
it's not clear

00:04:12.890 --> 00:04:15.780
which one of the 10
features just released

00:04:15.780 --> 00:04:17.329
caused the problem.

00:04:17.329 --> 00:04:21.510
So solving it
becomes much harder.

00:04:21.510 --> 00:04:24.360
From the SRE team
perspective, the team

00:04:24.360 --> 00:04:26.850
is working like crazy
to ship software,

00:04:26.850 --> 00:04:28.710
but it's never enough.

00:04:28.710 --> 00:04:34.140
So everybody's disappointed
and there's a ton of pressure.

00:04:34.140 --> 00:04:38.710
From a user's perspective,
features arrive slowly,

00:04:38.710 --> 00:04:43.980
and the system could
definitely be more stable.

00:04:43.980 --> 00:04:46.940
So we looked for solutions.

00:04:46.940 --> 00:04:50.810
Lucky for us, Netflix
was gracious enough

00:04:50.810 --> 00:04:54.020
to share both their
experience and the software

00:04:54.020 --> 00:04:59.520
they've built to solve this
problem, which is Spinnaker.

00:04:59.520 --> 00:05:03.290
So what exactly is Spinnaker?

00:05:03.290 --> 00:05:09.680
Well, it's an open source,
multi-cloud, continuous

00:05:09.680 --> 00:05:13.890
delivery platform for
deploying, releasing,

00:05:13.890 --> 00:05:19.980
software changes with high
velocity and confidence.

00:05:19.980 --> 00:05:24.680
So for us, Spinnaker is
essentially a shortcut

00:05:24.680 --> 00:05:26.390
to achieving what we want.

00:05:26.390 --> 00:05:32.150
And what we want is fast,
safe, and repeatable software

00:05:32.150 --> 00:05:36.140
delivery with easy rollbacks.

00:05:36.140 --> 00:05:38.690
So we deployed
Spinnaker throughout all

00:05:38.690 --> 00:05:41.390
of the development
teams at Waze.

00:05:41.390 --> 00:05:44.000
And this is how reality changed.

00:05:44.000 --> 00:05:49.610
As a developer, I now have
the feedback cycle I need.

00:05:49.610 --> 00:05:53.090
Because right now,
deployments are back

00:05:53.090 --> 00:05:55.850
to a single click operation.

00:05:55.850 --> 00:05:59.270
And it's all self-service.

00:05:59.270 --> 00:06:00.170
I can ship.

00:06:00.170 --> 00:06:01.650
I can get feedback.

00:06:01.650 --> 00:06:06.720
And the core of the matter is
that I have complete autonomy.

00:06:06.720 --> 00:06:09.300
And because I have
complete autonomy,

00:06:09.300 --> 00:06:13.140
I don't need to rely on
the SRE team anymore,

00:06:13.140 --> 00:06:17.400
which are now free to
work on system stability

00:06:17.400 --> 00:06:21.820
and scalability,
which is a good thing.

00:06:21.820 --> 00:06:23.472
So we have-- oh, sorry.

00:06:23.472 --> 00:06:27.180
And the best part is that our
users get features and bug

00:06:27.180 --> 00:06:29.440
fixes much quicker.

00:06:29.440 --> 00:06:32.010
So we have happier users.

00:06:32.010 --> 00:06:34.550
And because we're shipping
smaller amounts of features

00:06:34.550 --> 00:06:38.790
each time, the risk of
service disruption goes down.

00:06:38.790 --> 00:06:43.970
And when there is a problem,
solving it becomes much easier.

00:06:43.970 --> 00:06:47.750
When you see the immediate
feedback of your work

00:06:47.750 --> 00:06:50.150
and have complete
autonomy, it gives you

00:06:50.150 --> 00:06:55.610
more drive and motivation to go
even quicker, take more risks,

00:06:55.610 --> 00:06:58.850
and transform the service
to be even more amazing.

00:07:02.740 --> 00:07:06.090
So that explains things
on a super high level.

00:07:06.090 --> 00:07:08.130
If we drill down,
Spinnaker's power

00:07:08.130 --> 00:07:11.700
really comes from its
abstraction and powerful

00:07:11.700 --> 00:07:13.160
pipeline engine.

00:07:13.160 --> 00:07:17.580
It allows us to bridge between
multiple technology stacks.

00:07:17.580 --> 00:07:23.400
In our case, VMs and
Kubernetes and cloud providers.

00:07:23.400 --> 00:07:26.400
Its a sweet spot between
just the amount of

00:07:26.400 --> 00:07:29.680
abstraction on top
of each technology,

00:07:29.680 --> 00:07:34.070
but it is still able to
control each stack precisely.

00:07:34.070 --> 00:07:37.310
That's a bit contradictory,
but Spinnaker somehow

00:07:37.310 --> 00:07:40.800
manages to pull it off.

00:07:40.800 --> 00:07:43.560
Once applications
are using Spinnaker,

00:07:43.560 --> 00:07:46.740
everybody in the company has
this single pane of glass

00:07:46.740 --> 00:07:50.790
which can be used to perform
the daily work of deploying

00:07:50.790 --> 00:07:53.560
software.

00:07:53.560 --> 00:07:56.350
And the main point is that
it feels the same no matter

00:07:56.350 --> 00:07:58.330
what technology
stack you are on,

00:07:58.330 --> 00:08:01.690
whether you are on
VMs, Kubernetes,

00:08:01.690 --> 00:08:07.600
App Engine, or any cloud
provider supported.

00:08:07.600 --> 00:08:12.700
So life was good, for a while.

00:08:12.700 --> 00:08:15.640
As you well know, when
you solve one problem,

00:08:15.640 --> 00:08:20.780
two new problems are created,
and they were created.

00:08:20.780 --> 00:08:23.020
One of them was managing
this crazy amount

00:08:23.020 --> 00:08:25.330
of pipelines in production.

00:08:25.330 --> 00:08:29.090
And the other was that
we were moving too fast.

00:08:29.090 --> 00:08:33.299
Now, we wanted to move fast,
so why is that a problem?

00:08:33.299 --> 00:08:35.110
Well, it's a problem
because its risky,

00:08:35.110 --> 00:08:38.730
and we'll get to
that in a moment.

00:08:38.730 --> 00:08:42.350
So by now, we had over
1,000 deployments pipelines

00:08:42.350 --> 00:08:44.300
in production.

00:08:44.300 --> 00:08:47.810
All of them were managed
through the Spinnaker UI.

00:08:47.810 --> 00:08:51.770
And it became a huge pain
point for the teams to operate,

00:08:51.770 --> 00:08:54.380
because each of these
pipelines were essentially

00:08:54.380 --> 00:08:58.480
duplicates of one another, with
slight changes in between them.

00:08:58.480 --> 00:09:01.880
So for example, adding a stage
to all of these productions

00:09:01.880 --> 00:09:06.120
pipelines is too much of
a risk for production.

00:09:06.120 --> 00:09:10.240
There's actually no
one willing to do it.

00:09:10.240 --> 00:09:13.550
So we started looking
for solutions.

00:09:13.550 --> 00:09:18.880
And the idea was to build a
paved road deployment pipeline.

00:09:18.880 --> 00:09:21.310
Something that 80% of
the development teams

00:09:21.310 --> 00:09:26.730
could use and be happy
with, while the rest of them

00:09:26.730 --> 00:09:31.670
could change it and adapt it to
their needs, but still use it.

00:09:31.670 --> 00:09:34.890
What happened in the
past was that each team

00:09:34.890 --> 00:09:37.110
had their own set of pipelines.

00:09:37.110 --> 00:09:42.390
And when they had a good idea,
it remained local to that team.

00:09:42.390 --> 00:09:45.500
Now, with the paved
road pipeline templates,

00:09:45.500 --> 00:09:47.670
these changes are
done centrally.

00:09:47.670 --> 00:09:50.690
So all of these ideas
and best practices

00:09:50.690 --> 00:09:54.780
are available throughout
the entire company.

00:09:54.780 --> 00:09:57.980
So we started off with a
simple paved road pipeline.

00:09:57.980 --> 00:10:02.590
It has a bake, a
smoke test, or stage,

00:10:02.590 --> 00:10:04.630
and deploy to production.

00:10:04.630 --> 00:10:09.216
Each car represents one specific
version of an application.

00:10:14.460 --> 00:10:16.310
Now, from the
animation, you can see

00:10:16.310 --> 00:10:20.600
that what used to be 1,000
separate pipelines are now all

00:10:20.600 --> 00:10:24.740
using this paved road pipeline.

00:10:24.740 --> 00:10:27.530
And this encapsulates
the best practices

00:10:27.530 --> 00:10:31.920
we know of how to deliver
software to production.

00:10:31.920 --> 00:10:37.290
And it has also finally
become manageable.

00:10:37.290 --> 00:10:40.890
Remember that problem
of moving too fast?

00:10:40.890 --> 00:10:45.000
Well, this change essentially
raised the speed limit

00:10:45.000 --> 00:10:49.980
of the company from 60 miles
an hour to 600 miles an hour.

00:10:49.980 --> 00:10:53.710
And when you do that,
new risks will appear.

00:10:53.710 --> 00:10:59.910
Now, we wanted to move
extremely fast, but it's risky.

00:10:59.910 --> 00:11:03.450
So we needed more safeguards.

00:11:03.450 --> 00:11:07.650
In order to solve this problem,
we added a canary stage.

00:11:07.650 --> 00:11:09.512
We evolved our
paved road pipeline.

00:11:09.512 --> 00:11:10.470
Let's see how it looks.

00:11:19.272 --> 00:11:22.210
[LAUGHTER]

00:11:22.210 --> 00:11:26.245
So now she's checking
each version and deciding.

00:11:26.245 --> 00:11:26.744
OK.

00:11:30.010 --> 00:11:34.000
To do that, we simply duplicated
the previous paved road

00:11:34.000 --> 00:11:37.180
pipeline, add in a
canary stage, which

00:11:37.180 --> 00:11:41.150
Matt will talk about
later in depth,

00:11:41.150 --> 00:11:43.430
and then sent one
of the applications

00:11:43.430 --> 00:11:46.370
to the new paved road
pipeline, tested it.

00:11:46.370 --> 00:11:49.670
And once we were happy,
we disabled the old one

00:11:49.670 --> 00:11:52.700
and sent everybody
to the new road.

00:11:52.700 --> 00:11:55.640
So by now, developers
woke up one morning

00:11:55.640 --> 00:12:00.160
and had an option for a
safer deployment pipeline.

00:12:00.160 --> 00:12:03.870
They could now move from
a deploy and revert model

00:12:03.870 --> 00:12:05.200
to a canary model.

00:12:05.200 --> 00:12:08.510
And this means that
it can catch problems

00:12:08.510 --> 00:12:14.710
early on when it affects only a
slight percentage of the users.

00:12:14.710 --> 00:12:19.190
Let's say one team didn't
want to run all of the stages.

00:12:19.190 --> 00:12:21.060
Well, they could
simply bypass it.

00:12:21.060 --> 00:12:23.040
They could pick
and choose whatever

00:12:23.040 --> 00:12:25.470
stages they wanted to run.

00:12:25.470 --> 00:12:28.320
And if another team
wanted a person

00:12:28.320 --> 00:12:31.260
to approve deploying
to production,

00:12:31.260 --> 00:12:36.090
well, they could just inject
a new manual judgment stage

00:12:36.090 --> 00:12:38.415
right before the
deployment to production.

00:12:41.030 --> 00:12:43.160
Working with paved
road pipelines

00:12:43.160 --> 00:12:45.999
means that any
enhancements or fixes

00:12:45.999 --> 00:12:47.540
that we want to make
to the pipelines

00:12:47.540 --> 00:12:52.030
are immediately reflected
across the entire organization.

00:12:52.030 --> 00:12:54.620
And that makes it easy to
make decisions and have

00:12:54.620 --> 00:12:58.400
them automated throughout.

00:12:58.400 --> 00:13:01.810
There are some important
technical aspects to even.

00:13:01.810 --> 00:13:04.900
All of this is done as
code, with code reviews

00:13:04.900 --> 00:13:07.150
sent to teammates
before each change

00:13:07.150 --> 00:13:10.580
to this paved road system.

00:13:10.580 --> 00:13:15.940
So tracking and rolling
back is pretty easy.

00:13:15.940 --> 00:13:19.300
We have a few of these
paved roads at Waze.

00:13:19.300 --> 00:13:21.660
One is for microservices
and the other

00:13:21.660 --> 00:13:24.271
is for our data
layer, Cassandra.

00:13:27.160 --> 00:13:29.740
We actually run
blue-green deployments

00:13:29.740 --> 00:13:33.810
when upgrading or resizing
our Cassandras fleets.

00:13:33.810 --> 00:13:35.770
And we use pipeline
templates and Spinnaker

00:13:35.770 --> 00:13:38.140
to manage all of this.

00:13:38.140 --> 00:13:43.000
For example, if we want to stand
up a new multi-cloud Cassandra

00:13:43.000 --> 00:13:47.110
cluster, all it takes is
writing a tiny config file,

00:13:47.110 --> 00:13:49.930
generating the pipeline
templates for them,

00:13:49.930 --> 00:13:51.910
and running them in Spinnaker.

00:13:51.910 --> 00:13:55.090
Previously, this would have
taken way too much time

00:13:55.090 --> 00:13:56.950
and was prone to error.

00:13:56.950 --> 00:13:59.440
And now it's fully automatic.

00:13:59.440 --> 00:14:01.450
So for us, that's
pretty amazing.

00:14:04.130 --> 00:14:07.420
So that's more or less
the end of my story.

00:14:07.420 --> 00:14:09.930
Well, the Waze story.

00:14:09.930 --> 00:14:12.630
Today I believe that a
new developer at Waze

00:14:12.630 --> 00:14:16.180
has complete autonomy and
can focus on what matters,

00:14:16.180 --> 00:14:17.954
mainly thanks to Spinnaker.

00:14:20.920 --> 00:14:24.150
Waze has recently joined
the development efforts

00:14:24.150 --> 00:14:25.190
around Spinnaker.

00:14:25.190 --> 00:14:28.850
And we're specifically focused
on adding some SRE concepts,

00:14:28.850 --> 00:14:33.170
like service level
objectives and error budgets.

00:14:33.170 --> 00:14:36.320
I believe that once Spinnaker
is aware of an error

00:14:36.320 --> 00:14:39.470
budget for an application,
some interesting things

00:14:39.470 --> 00:14:41.220
can start to happen.

00:14:41.220 --> 00:14:45.260
For example, when a service
is almost out of error budget,

00:14:45.260 --> 00:14:49.400
the canary system could
automatically run longer

00:14:49.400 --> 00:14:52.040
to gain more confidence
in new versions,

00:14:52.040 --> 00:14:56.480
or perhaps something that was
previously fully automatic

00:14:56.480 --> 00:14:59.990
now has a manual judgment
stage kicking in because

00:14:59.990 --> 00:15:02.490
of the error budget.

00:15:02.490 --> 00:15:06.720
As happy as we are to be part
of the Spinnaker community,

00:15:06.720 --> 00:15:09.300
there wouldn't be a
Spinnaker community

00:15:09.300 --> 00:15:12.330
if it weren't for Andy
Glover and his excellent team

00:15:12.330 --> 00:15:15.630
at Netflix for
starting this project.

00:15:15.630 --> 00:15:19.390
We're lucky to have
Andy here today with us.

00:15:19.390 --> 00:15:20.708
Andy.

00:15:20.708 --> 00:15:22.704
[APPLAUSE]

00:15:22.704 --> 00:15:23.702
ANDY GLOVER: Hey, man.

00:15:23.702 --> 00:15:24.700
Thanks a lot.

00:15:24.700 --> 00:15:26.197
That was awesome.

00:15:29.700 --> 00:15:34.350
So I've got to warn you, my
slides aren't half as cool

00:15:34.350 --> 00:15:35.670
as Tom's.

00:15:35.670 --> 00:15:38.471
My name's Andy Glover and
I run delivery engineering

00:15:38.471 --> 00:15:38.970
at Netflix.

00:15:38.970 --> 00:15:40.260
We are the Spinnaker team.

00:15:40.260 --> 00:15:41.430
And I do work at Netflix.

00:15:41.430 --> 00:15:43.500
And I want to say
thanks to my good friend

00:15:43.500 --> 00:15:47.370
Matt who works at Google for
the phenomenal introduction

00:15:47.370 --> 00:15:49.120
earlier.

00:15:49.120 --> 00:15:52.440
So again, we're very
happy to be here.

00:15:52.440 --> 00:15:55.170
So at Netflix, we value speed.

00:15:55.170 --> 00:15:58.530
Indeed, speed of innovation
is our competitive advantage.

00:15:58.530 --> 00:16:00.660
We firmly believe that
if we aren't fast,

00:16:00.660 --> 00:16:03.060
our competitors
certainly will be.

00:16:03.060 --> 00:16:05.370
What's more, our members,
you all-- anyone here

00:16:05.370 --> 00:16:07.010
a Netflix member?

00:16:07.010 --> 00:16:07.510
A few.

00:16:07.510 --> 00:16:08.060
Thank you.

00:16:08.060 --> 00:16:08.893
Thank you very much.

00:16:08.893 --> 00:16:10.170
We love you.

00:16:10.170 --> 00:16:12.460
And we are firmly
convinced that you all

00:16:12.460 --> 00:16:14.830
demand that we move fast.

00:16:14.830 --> 00:16:16.390
When's the last
time you got upset

00:16:16.390 --> 00:16:18.705
when you ordered
something from Amazon

00:16:18.705 --> 00:16:21.590
and it didn't
arrive the same day?

00:16:21.590 --> 00:16:24.610
So if we don't move fast,
we're pretty sure that you'll

00:16:24.610 --> 00:16:26.490
go to our competitors.

00:16:26.490 --> 00:16:29.680
So whether it's to address
a bug, add new features,

00:16:29.680 --> 00:16:34.180
or even add new content, you
name it, if we don't move fast

00:16:34.180 --> 00:16:36.700
and meet your
expectations, we're

00:16:36.700 --> 00:16:38.230
convinced you'll go elsewhere.

00:16:38.230 --> 00:16:42.220
We also firmly believe
that the faster we learn,

00:16:42.220 --> 00:16:43.990
the faster we
evolve and continue

00:16:43.990 --> 00:16:45.100
to win moments of truth.

00:16:49.400 --> 00:16:52.310
Netflix is a global company.

00:16:52.310 --> 00:16:57.380
Our service is used by more than
130 million people every day.

00:16:57.380 --> 00:17:00.650
And we're available in
almost 200 countries.

00:17:00.650 --> 00:17:04.460
And to facilitate our growth,
we have a global infrastructure.

00:17:04.460 --> 00:17:07.640
We leverage AWS and
operate in multiple regions

00:17:07.640 --> 00:17:09.270
around the world.

00:17:09.270 --> 00:17:12.349
Moreover, we also leverage a
commercial content delivery

00:17:12.349 --> 00:17:15.440
network, as well as our own
fleet of globally distributed

00:17:15.440 --> 00:17:19.450
servers for content distribution
called open connect appliances.

00:17:27.819 --> 00:17:29.610
So operating at
this level of scale

00:17:29.610 --> 00:17:33.840
requires sophisticated
tooling that ultimately

00:17:33.840 --> 00:17:36.060
makes it easy for
development teams

00:17:36.060 --> 00:17:38.130
to move fast with a high
degree of confidence,

00:17:38.130 --> 00:17:39.810
as you saw with Waze.

00:17:39.810 --> 00:17:42.795
And so that's why we built a
continuous delivery platform,

00:17:42.795 --> 00:17:45.750
or excuse me, a continuous
delivery and infrastructure

00:17:45.750 --> 00:17:50.094
management platform, which we
accordingly named Spinnaker.

00:17:50.094 --> 00:17:51.510
And the key thing
is Spinnaker was

00:17:51.510 --> 00:17:53.880
built leveraging the
myriad lessons learned

00:17:53.880 --> 00:17:55.710
from multiple
systems that enabled

00:17:55.710 --> 00:17:59.710
continuous delivery at Netflix
over the past number of years.

00:17:59.710 --> 00:18:01.720
And so orchestrating
the delivery

00:18:01.720 --> 00:18:04.360
of software assets via
Spinnaker facilitates

00:18:04.360 --> 00:18:07.330
moving fast at a global scale.

00:18:07.330 --> 00:18:10.540
It has accelerated
our time to market.

00:18:10.540 --> 00:18:14.770
Spinnaker empowers teams to make
rapid changes with confidence,

00:18:14.770 --> 00:18:18.490
and thus enables a focus on
building the right product

00:18:18.490 --> 00:18:20.230
for you all.

00:18:20.230 --> 00:18:24.480
It has also improved developer
productivity and efficiency.

00:18:29.480 --> 00:18:33.940
So yeah, that's my
paved road slide.

00:18:33.940 --> 00:18:34.720
Thanks, Tom.

00:18:34.720 --> 00:18:36.272
Appreciate it.

00:18:36.272 --> 00:18:41.470
[LAUGHS] So yeah,
Spinnaker is a platform

00:18:41.470 --> 00:18:43.950
that enables all the benefits
of continuous delivery.

00:18:43.950 --> 00:18:46.750
And accordingly, it
acts as a paved road

00:18:46.750 --> 00:18:49.900
for making it painless to
take ideas and put them

00:18:49.900 --> 00:18:52.240
in front of users rapidly.

00:18:52.240 --> 00:18:56.380
This paved road enables
teams at Netflix

00:18:56.380 --> 00:19:02.250
to focus on their charters and
not on the nuances of delivery.

00:19:02.250 --> 00:19:05.400
So ultimately, Spinnaker makes
developers' lives easier.

00:19:08.710 --> 00:19:11.590
And because its an
extensible platform,

00:19:11.590 --> 00:19:13.600
Spinnaker provides
a means to plug

00:19:13.600 --> 00:19:16.780
into a greater infrastructure
of integrated tools

00:19:16.780 --> 00:19:20.990
in other platforms, like
Chaos Monkey, squeeze testing,

00:19:20.990 --> 00:19:23.980
which we call Citrus, and
automated canary analysis,

00:19:23.980 --> 00:19:27.629
which Tom alluded to
with his awesome slides,

00:19:27.629 --> 00:19:29.920
and which I believe there's
going to be a demo as well.

00:19:29.920 --> 00:19:32.310
We call it Kayenta.

00:19:32.310 --> 00:19:35.010
And so developers
who use Spinnaker,

00:19:35.010 --> 00:19:37.410
they get all these tools
for free without having

00:19:37.410 --> 00:19:41.160
to write all the glue code to
switch together a complex--

00:19:41.160 --> 00:19:43.950
or to, excuse me, stitch
together a complex delivery

00:19:43.950 --> 00:19:46.476
process, like people were
doing before Spinnaker.

00:19:50.850 --> 00:19:54.840
At Netflix, there aren't
any centralized DevOps teams

00:19:54.840 --> 00:19:57.310
or release teams.

00:19:57.310 --> 00:20:00.620
Teams at Netflix own
their own destiny,

00:20:00.620 --> 00:20:02.840
building their own
pipelines in Spinnaker,

00:20:02.840 --> 00:20:05.740
and managing their
apps in production.

00:20:05.740 --> 00:20:09.660
They leverage what's available
out of the box in Spinnaker,

00:20:09.660 --> 00:20:10.230
but--

00:20:10.230 --> 00:20:11.410
and this is key--

00:20:11.410 --> 00:20:13.960
they're free to build
on top of Spinnaker,

00:20:13.960 --> 00:20:16.720
adding their needs so
as to efficiently reap

00:20:16.720 --> 00:20:19.101
the benefits of
continuous delivery.

00:20:24.510 --> 00:20:26.960
So the needs of various
teams at Netflix

00:20:26.960 --> 00:20:30.170
has resulted in
powerful extensions

00:20:30.170 --> 00:20:33.620
within Spinnaker that enable
not only rapid and reliable

00:20:33.620 --> 00:20:35.390
delivery to AWS--

00:20:35.390 --> 00:20:38.720
anyone here heard of AWS?

00:20:38.720 --> 00:20:40.320
Had to sneak that in--

00:20:40.320 --> 00:20:44.150
but also to Akamai, which is
a content delivery network,

00:20:44.150 --> 00:20:46.220
and to our own container
cloud called Titus.

00:20:46.220 --> 00:20:48.211
You can think of it a
lot like Kubernetes.

00:20:51.440 --> 00:20:54.050
But the innovations
don't stop there.

00:20:54.050 --> 00:20:56.750
Spinnaker has also been
extended to facilitate delivery

00:20:56.750 --> 00:21:01.300
of firmware updates to that
fleet of global servers

00:21:01.300 --> 00:21:03.590
that I called open
connect appliances, which

00:21:03.590 --> 00:21:06.380
are distributed across
the world in data centers

00:21:06.380 --> 00:21:07.470
closest to where you live.

00:21:07.470 --> 00:21:09.800
So when you actually hit
Play, the video itself is

00:21:09.800 --> 00:21:12.990
streamed to you from that
box, not from the cloud,

00:21:12.990 --> 00:21:15.200
so to speak, AWS.

00:21:15.200 --> 00:21:20.580
Spinnaker enables the safe
manipulation of feature flags.

00:21:20.580 --> 00:21:24.540
That is, you can create a
complex delivery pipeline

00:21:24.540 --> 00:21:28.290
for changing runtime
data in production

00:21:28.290 --> 00:21:32.010
without shooting
yourself in the foot.

00:21:32.010 --> 00:21:34.770
And furthermore,
Spinnaker now enables

00:21:34.770 --> 00:21:39.330
the delivery and management
of libraries across Netflix.

00:21:39.330 --> 00:21:41.790
So you can imagine there
are teams at Netflix that

00:21:41.790 --> 00:21:44.400
potentially don't, or
do not, own a service

00:21:44.400 --> 00:21:45.560
that facilitates streaming.

00:21:45.560 --> 00:21:49.230
They ultimately own a library
that facilitates streaming.

00:21:49.230 --> 00:21:51.570
And they, too, now
are using Spinnaker

00:21:51.570 --> 00:21:54.332
to effect delivery of those
libraries to other teams

00:21:54.332 --> 00:21:56.040
and managing those
libraries once they're

00:21:56.040 --> 00:21:57.416
in the infrastructure.

00:22:05.520 --> 00:22:08.670
As Tom said, Spinnaker
is open source

00:22:08.670 --> 00:22:13.060
with a strong and vibrant
community, including Netflix,

00:22:13.060 --> 00:22:17.940
Google, or Woogle,
Waze, but also

00:22:17.940 --> 00:22:20.680
folks like Target, who's
here at this conference,

00:22:20.680 --> 00:22:24.120
my good friends from Pivotal,
Under Armor, Microsoft.

00:22:24.120 --> 00:22:26.700
And that's just naming a few of
the large companies investing

00:22:26.700 --> 00:22:29.110
in this platform.

00:22:29.110 --> 00:22:32.360
Everyone is innovating
within the platform.

00:22:32.360 --> 00:22:34.780
So not only does Spinnaker
integrate with major cloud

00:22:34.780 --> 00:22:38.530
vendors, but there are
integrations with OpenStack,

00:22:38.530 --> 00:22:44.810
Pivotal Cloud Foundry, DCOS, and
even Oracle Bare Metal Cloud.

00:22:44.810 --> 00:22:51.820
What's more, the community is
building new pipeline stages,

00:22:51.820 --> 00:22:56.020
command line tools for
interacting with and managing

00:22:56.020 --> 00:23:02.480
Spinnaker, and even new
Kayenta metric sources,

00:23:02.480 --> 00:23:05.141
like Stackdriver and Datadog.

00:23:05.141 --> 00:23:06.890
This will all make
more sense once you see

00:23:06.890 --> 00:23:10.340
the demo of canary analysis.

00:23:10.340 --> 00:23:12.350
And that's just some
of the innovations

00:23:12.350 --> 00:23:17.640
that this community is building
within the Spinnaker ecosystem.

00:23:17.640 --> 00:23:20.310
And so I think all of
us within the community

00:23:20.310 --> 00:23:24.450
would agree that rapid and
reliable global software

00:23:24.450 --> 00:23:30.080
delivery has never been
more easy than it is today.

00:23:30.080 --> 00:23:33.190
And with that, I will turn it
over to my good friend Matt

00:23:33.190 --> 00:23:36.020
from Google.

00:23:36.020 --> 00:23:39.020
[APPLAUSE]

00:23:44.506 --> 00:23:45.630
MATT DUFTLER: Thanks, Andy.

