Thanks.

00:24:36.150 --> 00:24:38.810
All right, so it's
going to push a change.

00:24:38.810 --> 00:24:41.390
The idea here is that
we'll let it work.

00:24:41.390 --> 00:24:42.930
It'll take maybe
10 minutes or so.

00:24:42.930 --> 00:24:44.971
We'll come back and poke
around the configuration

00:24:44.971 --> 00:24:47.300
and the execution
of that pipeline.

00:24:47.300 --> 00:24:49.710
It's a live change against Waze.

00:24:49.710 --> 00:24:51.800
Any of you planning
on skipping out

00:24:51.800 --> 00:24:55.370
early, bad time to use Waze
to get out of San Francisco.

00:24:55.370 --> 00:24:58.350
But that's on you.

00:24:58.350 --> 00:25:02.040
All right, so in the past,
Google's disclosed many numbers

00:25:02.040 --> 00:25:03.870
about our internal
source control system.

00:25:03.870 --> 00:25:05.610
And these are
incredible numbers.

00:25:05.610 --> 00:25:07.950
My data is out of
date, but things

00:25:07.950 --> 00:25:10.650
like over a billion
files, tens of millions

00:25:10.650 --> 00:25:13.260
of commits, tens of
thousands of software

00:25:13.260 --> 00:25:16.770
developers, all working out
of the same giant repository.

00:25:16.770 --> 00:25:20.580
I want to talk about a
different set of numbers now.

00:25:20.580 --> 00:25:23.160
The goal here is not
really to leave you

00:25:23.160 --> 00:25:25.890
with an impression about
Google's internal scale

00:25:25.890 --> 00:25:28.200
or complexity, but more
about just our commitment

00:25:28.200 --> 00:25:30.880
to the Spinnaker project and
the surrounding community.

00:25:30.880 --> 00:25:33.089
So I'm part of the
Spinnaker team.

00:25:33.089 --> 00:25:34.380
We work full time on Spinnaker.

00:25:34.380 --> 00:25:37.350
In the last year or so, we've
more than doubled in size.

00:25:37.350 --> 00:25:42.570
We have 13 full time software
engineers, full time product

00:25:42.570 --> 00:25:44.940
manager, a technical
program manager,

00:25:44.940 --> 00:25:47.100
and a dedicated
engineering manager.

00:25:47.100 --> 00:25:48.000
He brought stickers.

00:25:48.000 --> 00:25:50.490
He's around here somewhere.

00:25:50.490 --> 00:25:52.980
What we're doing, these are
some of the things we work on.

00:25:52.980 --> 00:25:55.380
We support customers and users.

00:25:55.380 --> 00:25:58.740
Not all of the users we work
with are Google customers.

00:25:58.740 --> 00:25:59.820
Many are, but not all.

00:25:59.820 --> 00:26:02.789
We're in the Slack channel all
the time just helping people.

00:26:02.789 --> 00:26:04.830
It's good for the community
and it's good for us,

00:26:04.830 --> 00:26:06.871
because we find out what
people are interested in

00:26:06.871 --> 00:26:09.100
and what's important
to the company.

00:26:09.100 --> 00:26:10.920
So it lets us focus.

00:26:10.920 --> 00:26:13.230
We built out a large
release workflow

00:26:13.230 --> 00:26:16.170
for building, testing, and
releasing Spinnaker itself.

00:26:16.170 --> 00:26:19.440
The project is 9, 10,
11, I don't know how many

00:26:19.440 --> 00:26:21.000
independent microservices.

00:26:21.000 --> 00:26:24.030
It's a big, complicated project.

00:26:24.030 --> 00:26:26.340
Before we built this
release workflow,

00:26:26.340 --> 00:26:28.740
folks were pretty much
just running it at head.

00:26:28.740 --> 00:26:30.990
Clone all the repos, figure
out how to configure it,

00:26:30.990 --> 00:26:34.080
fight YAML, run it,
ask questions on Slack.

00:26:34.080 --> 00:26:37.380
We built out a process to do
regularly scheduled integration

00:26:37.380 --> 00:26:38.880
tests and have
versioned releases

00:26:38.880 --> 00:26:41.250
across all these
independent services,

00:26:41.250 --> 00:26:45.090
including change logs, pegging
changes to future versions,

00:26:45.090 --> 00:26:45.870
and so on.

00:26:45.870 --> 00:26:50.064
The goal there was to
really make it easier

00:26:50.064 --> 00:26:50.980
to install and manage.

00:26:50.980 --> 00:26:52.771
And we'll talk more
about that in a second.

00:26:52.771 --> 00:26:54.660
But also to give
confidence to enterprises

00:26:54.660 --> 00:26:57.600
that it's a real project,
real product, has a version.

00:26:57.600 --> 00:26:59.520
You can count on it.

00:26:59.520 --> 00:27:01.860
We'll release patch releases
when important things

00:27:01.860 --> 00:27:04.630
are discovered and so on.

00:27:04.630 --> 00:27:09.740
On the topic of installing and
configuring Spinnaker, it's--

00:27:09.740 --> 00:27:11.690
well, the idea is that
you can install it,

00:27:11.690 --> 00:27:12.648
you can get it running.

00:27:12.648 --> 00:27:15.200
And then when you have
an issue, in the past,

00:27:15.200 --> 00:27:18.320
we had to go and say, try this
YAML file, make this change,

00:27:18.320 --> 00:27:19.140
and so on.

00:27:19.140 --> 00:27:21.800
So we built a tool to make
that easier called Halyard.

00:27:21.800 --> 00:27:23.425
We'll talk more about
that in a second,

00:27:23.425 --> 00:27:25.549
but I think it's a pretty
good example of something

00:27:25.549 --> 00:27:28.130
we built entirely out in the
open in the open source that

00:27:28.130 --> 00:27:30.440
makes everybody's lives
working with Spinnaker quite

00:27:30.440 --> 00:27:31.400
a bit easier.

00:27:31.400 --> 00:27:33.350
We also work on internal
usage of Spinnaker.

00:27:33.350 --> 00:27:35.210
There's some Google
teams pushing

00:27:35.210 --> 00:27:38.900
changes, an internal Google
infrastructure using Spinnaker.

00:27:38.900 --> 00:27:44.620
And we'll talk more about that
in the coming months as well.

00:27:44.620 --> 00:27:46.459
So the obvious question--

00:27:46.459 --> 00:27:48.000
well, not who is
that, because that's

00:27:48.000 --> 00:27:50.310
somebody who works at
Waze, because I've seen him

00:27:50.310 --> 00:27:52.920
when I visited, but why?

00:27:52.920 --> 00:27:55.362
Why such an investment
in the Spinnaker project?

00:27:55.362 --> 00:27:56.570
So there are lots of reasons.

00:27:56.570 --> 00:27:58.920
The first is that there are
primitives in operations

00:27:58.920 --> 00:28:02.340
that Spinnaker defines,
align really, really

00:28:02.340 --> 00:28:04.780
well with our internal
best practices.

00:28:04.780 --> 00:28:07.770
Things that folks do on internal
proprietary Google systems

00:28:07.770 --> 00:28:10.230
match up perfectly well
with what Spinnaker

00:28:10.230 --> 00:28:13.770
is bringing to the community.

00:28:13.770 --> 00:28:15.870
Another good reason
for us, convergence.

00:28:15.870 --> 00:28:17.970
Whether it's open
source software, state

00:28:17.970 --> 00:28:19.560
of the art software
like Spinnaker,

00:28:19.560 --> 00:28:22.230
or our various cloud
offerings that GCP is offering

00:28:22.230 --> 00:28:24.750
to customers, the more
we can align those things

00:28:24.750 --> 00:28:27.810
with what we do internally,
the better for customers,

00:28:27.810 --> 00:28:31.670
the better for hiring new
folks at Google, and so on.

00:28:31.670 --> 00:28:34.080
It's an all around good thing.

00:28:34.080 --> 00:28:36.226
Tom talked a bit
about abstractions.

00:28:36.226 --> 00:28:38.100
The idea with Spinnaker
isn't that it creates

00:28:38.100 --> 00:28:41.280
perfect abstractions over every
underlying cloud platform,

00:28:41.280 --> 00:28:42.870
but that it creates
a commonality,

00:28:42.870 --> 00:28:44.910
so you have a
consistent experience.

00:28:44.910 --> 00:28:47.460
It's just consistent enough
that you can find your way,

00:28:47.460 --> 00:28:49.860
and hopefully, each
development team

00:28:49.860 --> 00:28:52.560
doesn't have to become an
expert on every cloud platform

00:28:52.560 --> 00:28:54.850
that they're targeting.

00:28:54.850 --> 00:28:57.810
The operations themselves
are abstractions for, and it

00:28:57.810 --> 00:29:00.240
does help you get out
of the weeds in terms

00:29:00.240 --> 00:29:03.870
of baking images, and pushing
new versions of your software

00:29:03.870 --> 00:29:07.122
out into production, doing
rollbacks, managing traffic,

00:29:07.122 --> 00:29:07.830
running canaries.

00:29:07.830 --> 00:29:11.087
All of these things are defined
as first class primitives,

00:29:11.087 --> 00:29:13.170
and they let you think at
a slightly higher level,

00:29:13.170 --> 00:29:15.720
rather than fighting
every nuance and detail

00:29:15.720 --> 00:29:18.430
of the underlying platforms.

00:29:18.430 --> 00:29:22.530
Lastly, Google's tied at the
hip with open source software.

00:29:22.530 --> 00:29:24.840
We contribute to tons
of open source projects.

00:29:24.840 --> 00:29:27.030
We use open source
software ourselves

00:29:27.030 --> 00:29:28.660
throughout the Google stack.

00:29:28.660 --> 00:29:29.610
So it's everywhere.

00:29:29.610 --> 00:29:32.490
Spinnaker is really just
one more example of Google

00:29:32.490 --> 00:29:36.840
being part of the broader
open source community.

00:29:36.840 --> 00:29:39.060
So in terms of Google's
involvement with Spinnaker,

00:29:39.060 --> 00:29:43.680
it started maybe four or so
years ago, late summer 2014.

00:29:43.680 --> 00:29:47.250
One person working part time on
the project as a 20% project.

00:29:47.250 --> 00:29:49.140
And it took off from there.

00:29:49.140 --> 00:29:52.760
We started off with
adding support for GCE,

00:29:52.760 --> 00:29:55.840
and then Kubernetes, and GKE,
and App Engine, and so on.

00:29:55.840 --> 00:29:58.140
And as each platform
becomes available,

00:29:58.140 --> 00:29:59.497
we've continued to add support.

00:29:59.497 --> 00:30:01.830
And then we've given a lot
of that back to the community

00:30:01.830 --> 00:30:03.690
by helping other
folks, some of whom

00:30:03.690 --> 00:30:05.670
look like competitors,
some are competitors,

00:30:05.670 --> 00:30:07.920
but we're all kind of working
together out in the open

00:30:07.920 --> 00:30:09.961
and sharing what we all
know about adding support

00:30:09.961 --> 00:30:11.170
for additional platforms.

00:30:11.170 --> 00:30:14.290
And that's been
pretty rewarding.

00:30:14.290 --> 00:30:17.635
So I wrote make it easy
to install and manage.

00:30:17.635 --> 00:30:19.510
And I remembered there's
going to be a video,

00:30:19.510 --> 00:30:21.590
and I crossed it out
and I wrote easier.

00:30:21.590 --> 00:30:23.620
It's not easy, but
it's hopefully easier.

00:30:23.620 --> 00:30:27.130
This is Halyard that I was
describing a moment ago.

00:30:27.130 --> 00:30:28.510
It's much better than it was.

00:30:28.510 --> 00:30:29.944
It's definitely easier.

00:30:29.944 --> 00:30:30.610
It's not simple.

00:30:30.610 --> 00:30:33.640
It's a big, complicated thing.

00:30:33.640 --> 00:30:35.670
That's just all there is to it.

00:30:35.670 --> 00:30:38.530
The types of organizations
that adopt Spinnaker

00:30:38.530 --> 00:30:41.740
are large organizations,
typically,

00:30:41.740 --> 00:30:45.010
maybe serving multiple teams
across multiple projects,

00:30:45.010 --> 00:30:47.140
multiple cloud
platforms, with lots

00:30:47.140 --> 00:30:48.980
of complexity and variability.

00:30:48.980 --> 00:30:52.120
And that requires a pretty
sophisticated platform.

00:30:52.120 --> 00:30:54.400
Spinnaker itself has all
these individual services

00:30:54.400 --> 00:30:57.310
that can each horizontally
scale as they need to,

00:30:57.310 --> 00:31:00.100
and they all kind of cooperate
to produce this platform.

00:31:00.100 --> 00:31:02.491
So it's not trivial
to install or manage.

00:31:02.491 --> 00:31:04.240
But Halyard lets you
do it, lets you do it

00:31:04.240 --> 00:31:07.780
on VMs, on containers, in an
atomic fashion or a distributed

00:31:07.780 --> 00:31:10.430
fashion, handles no
downtime upgrades,

00:31:10.430 --> 00:31:14.510
and flipping every option on and
off, and everything in between.

00:31:14.510 --> 00:31:16.090
And I wrote advanced topics.

00:31:16.090 --> 00:31:18.090
I wasn't sure what to
call it, but the idea here

00:31:18.090 --> 00:31:19.600
is that beyond
sort of making sure

00:31:19.600 --> 00:31:22.515
all the table stakes are there
to make enterprises happy.

00:31:22.515 --> 00:31:23.890
We work on a lot
of things that I

00:31:23.890 --> 00:31:27.160
would describe as adding in
best practices and smarts

00:31:27.160 --> 00:31:28.450
into the platform.

00:31:28.450 --> 00:31:30.359
Just as we learn
things over time,

00:31:30.359 --> 00:31:31.900
we try to build more
and more of that

00:31:31.900 --> 00:31:34.360
in so companies can
adopt this, encourage

00:31:34.360 --> 00:31:36.620
their own teams to
follow best practices,

00:31:36.620 --> 00:31:39.370
but also share back what we've
learned so not everybody has

00:31:39.370 --> 00:31:43.000
to create a canary analysis
service from scratch, which

00:31:43.000 --> 00:31:46.169
is what we're going
to talk about next.

00:31:46.169 --> 00:31:46.960
So this is Kayenta.

00:31:46.960 --> 00:31:48.430
Think Andy mentioned it.

00:31:48.430 --> 00:31:50.450
It's an automated
canary analysis service.

00:31:50.450 --> 00:31:52.040
That's a mouthful.

00:31:52.040 --> 00:31:54.910
It's a recently released
Spinnaker service.

00:31:54.910 --> 00:31:57.910
We partnered with another
team at Netflix, part of which

00:31:57.910 --> 00:31:59.970
is now, I think, part
of the Spinnaker team

00:31:59.970 --> 00:32:01.290
proper at Netflix--

00:32:01.290 --> 00:32:06.230
DET team, Delivery
Engineering Team at Netflix.

00:32:06.230 --> 00:32:08.860
So we partnered to take
a service that they've

00:32:08.860 --> 00:32:10.420
been running for
many years inside

00:32:10.420 --> 00:32:13.000
of Netflix, which was like
battle-hardened through quite

00:32:13.000 --> 00:32:17.380
a bit of adoption there, called
ACA, and kind of reimagine it

00:32:17.380 --> 00:32:20.140
and evolve it to be a bit
more open and extensible

00:32:20.140 --> 00:32:22.780
and support a broader
set of use cases.

00:32:22.780 --> 00:32:23.905
And that's what Kayenta is.

00:32:23.905 --> 00:32:25.988
And we did it with the
team that have been working

00:32:25.988 --> 00:32:27.280
on that service all along.

00:32:27.280 --> 00:32:29.590
It was open sourced
a few months back.

00:32:29.590 --> 00:32:32.670
Some of the key things we wanted
to accomplish out of the gate

00:32:32.670 --> 00:32:34.090
were to make sure
that it supports

00:32:34.090 --> 00:32:36.545
all of the cloud platforms
that Spinnaker itself supports.

00:32:36.545 --> 00:32:39.550
So it's not geared towards any
one platform in particular.

00:32:39.550 --> 00:32:41.170
So right out of
the gate, the day

00:32:41.170 --> 00:32:43.780
it was released, it
could be used with GCE,

00:32:43.780 --> 00:32:47.200
with AWS, with Kubernetes,
every other supported platform.

00:32:47.200 --> 00:32:49.450
It doesn't really have an
opinion about the underlying

00:32:49.450 --> 00:32:51.040
platform.

00:32:51.040 --> 00:32:53.080
In addition, it supports
an extensible set

00:32:53.080 --> 00:32:54.070
of metrics services.

00:32:54.070 --> 00:32:57.160
So we released it as
support for Stackdriver,

00:32:57.160 --> 00:32:59.950
with support for Atlas, which
is Netflix's own time series

00:32:59.950 --> 00:33:03.970
database, support for
Prometheus, the Armory folks.

00:33:03.970 --> 00:33:05.580
I saw some of them
bouncing around.

00:33:05.580 --> 00:33:07.780
Added support for Datadog.

00:33:07.780 --> 00:33:10.384
A community member added
support for Influx DB right

00:33:10.384 --> 00:33:11.300
after it was released.

00:33:11.300 --> 00:33:15.250
So it's a growing set of
metric services it supports.

00:33:15.250 --> 00:33:16.990
All the way up through
UI integration.

00:33:16.990 --> 00:33:18.239
It's pretty cool to work with.

00:33:18.239 --> 00:33:20.590
And we'll see some
of that in a moment.

00:33:20.590 --> 00:33:22.315
Lastly, pluggable canary judges.

00:33:22.315 --> 00:33:25.280
The initially supported judge
is the one that was in use

00:33:25.280 --> 00:33:27.245
at Netflix, is still in use.

00:33:27.245 --> 00:33:28.870
I think it's just
called the ACA judge,

00:33:28.870 --> 00:33:30.497
but we can call it
the Netflix judge.

00:33:30.497 --> 00:33:32.080
There are other
judges in development.

00:33:32.080 --> 00:33:33.790
Google has some
judges internally

00:33:33.790 --> 00:33:35.680
that we're working
on integrating.

00:33:35.680 --> 00:33:37.540
But the idea is it's
extensible and people

00:33:37.540 --> 00:33:39.020
can do what makes sense there.

00:33:39.020 --> 00:33:44.000
You're not stuck to one
particular approach.

00:33:44.000 --> 00:33:46.270
So this is going to
be-- it's kind of quick,

00:33:46.270 --> 00:33:48.880
and it's just really a taste
of what Kayenta offers.

00:33:48.880 --> 00:33:51.542
We think it's a very good
example of the kinds of things

00:33:51.542 --> 00:33:53.250
we're doing, really,
to push the envelope

00:33:53.250 --> 00:33:54.984
in the continuous
delivery space.

00:33:54.984 --> 00:33:56.650
There are webinars
and things that we've

00:33:56.650 --> 00:34:01.390
done that you can find online
for more details about Kayenta.

00:34:01.390 --> 00:34:04.760
All right, so this brings
us back to the pipeline.

00:34:04.760 --> 00:34:07.810
So what that pipeline does, and
we'll look at it in a second,

00:34:07.810 --> 00:34:10.440
it orchestrates a canary
release for a particular Waze

00:34:10.440 --> 00:34:13.120
service when a new
image becomes available.

00:34:13.120 --> 00:34:15.442
The idea with
canarying changes is

00:34:15.442 --> 00:34:17.650
that it really should be
used for any kind of change.

00:34:17.650 --> 00:34:20.270
It can be a binary change,
like a new image is available.

00:34:20.270 --> 00:34:22.712
It can be a config change,
an infrastructure change.

00:34:22.712 --> 00:34:24.670
But the bottom line is
you have some change you

00:34:24.670 --> 00:34:26.878
want to make against something
running in production,

00:34:26.878 --> 00:34:28.630
and you want to do
it in a safe way.

00:34:28.630 --> 00:34:30.504
You want to determine
in an automated

00:34:30.504 --> 00:34:34.810
of a fashion as possible whether
it's a safe change or not.

00:34:34.810 --> 00:34:37.389
And you want to make sure
to have a very narrow blast

00:34:37.389 --> 00:34:38.170
radius.

00:34:38.170 --> 00:34:40.628
And then over time, as you gain
confidence in that release,

00:34:40.628 --> 00:34:43.909
you roll it out to a
broader and broader scope.

00:34:43.909 --> 00:34:46.090
So this particular one is
just a new binary image

00:34:46.090 --> 00:34:47.840
becoming available.

00:34:47.840 --> 00:34:50.260
This is the service
that it affects.

00:34:50.260 --> 00:34:52.300
It's a Waze text
to speech service.

00:34:52.300 --> 00:34:54.429
It generates real
time audio when you're

00:34:54.429 --> 00:34:55.730
using Waze for navigation.

00:34:55.730 --> 00:34:57.070
It's a global service.

00:34:57.070 --> 00:35:00.430
It's available behind a global
load balancer, an HTTPS load

00:35:00.430 --> 00:35:02.067
balancer.

00:35:02.067 --> 00:35:03.400
It's requested miles in advance.

00:35:03.400 --> 00:35:06.160
So hopefully, no Waze
users were harmed

00:35:06.160 --> 00:35:07.490
in the making of this demo.

00:35:07.490 --> 00:35:09.615
But it's not really sensitive
to latency because it

00:35:09.615 --> 00:35:10.840
happens way in advance.

00:35:10.840 --> 00:35:14.620
What we're doing here is we took
the running production image

00:35:14.620 --> 00:35:15.370
and we changed it.

00:35:15.370 --> 00:35:17.290
And we changed it to
artificially introduce

00:35:17.290 --> 00:35:19.390
some latency into the backend.

00:35:19.390 --> 00:35:22.780
So the load balancer that's
forwarding traffic to all

00:35:22.780 --> 00:35:25.390
of these individual
backends is also

00:35:25.390 --> 00:35:28.366
reporting to Stackdriver metrics
on things like backend latency.

00:35:28.366 --> 00:35:29.740
And in this case,
we artificially

00:35:29.740 --> 00:35:31.721
increase that latency,
so the metrics

00:35:31.721 --> 00:35:33.220
that are recorded
in Stackdriver are

00:35:33.220 --> 00:35:35.740
going to show a higher number
for that particular metric.

00:35:35.740 --> 00:35:38.590
And then we configured an
extremely simple canary

00:35:38.590 --> 00:35:42.400
analysis that looks at just
that one backend latency metric

00:35:42.400 --> 00:35:44.830
and tries to determine, with
some automated statistical

00:35:44.830 --> 00:35:48.320
tests, whether there's a
significant difference there.

00:35:48.320 --> 00:35:51.400
And this is the basic path
of automated canary analysis.

00:35:51.400 --> 00:35:53.920
You find the strong
indicators of safety

00:35:53.920 --> 00:35:56.626
for your particular use
case, your application.

00:35:56.626 --> 00:35:58.000
You configure the
canary analysis

00:35:58.000 --> 00:35:59.010
to look those things up.

00:35:59.010 --> 00:36:01.120
You make sure they're being
published from your application

00:36:01.120 --> 00:36:02.320
and your infrastructure.

00:36:02.320 --> 00:36:06.040
And then you apply, at runtime,
the statistical techniques,

00:36:06.040 --> 00:36:07.570
which we can talk more about.

00:36:07.570 --> 00:36:09.270
And then a scoring
strategy is applied.

00:36:09.270 --> 00:36:11.770
And it's compared to thresholds
that you define for your use

00:36:11.770 --> 00:36:13.150
case, your pipelines.

00:36:13.150 --> 00:36:16.300
And then it's treated as a
gate for that deployment, OK?

00:36:16.300 --> 00:36:18.500
In this case, it's
a trivial example.

00:36:18.500 --> 00:36:21.030
While it's running against
live systems, we gave it,

00:36:21.030 --> 00:36:22.870
I don't know, six
minutes to warm up

00:36:22.870 --> 00:36:24.940
and four minutes of runtime.

00:36:24.940 --> 00:36:27.290
So we're going to get four
one-minute data points.

00:36:27.290 --> 00:36:29.140
It's a very small set of data.

00:36:29.140 --> 00:36:31.180
It's just for demonstration.

00:36:31.180 --> 00:36:33.130
In experimenting
with it, we ended up

00:36:33.130 --> 00:36:35.050
turning up the sensitivity
of the judge a bit,

00:36:35.050 --> 00:36:37.300
because the judge
would basically say,

00:36:37.300 --> 00:36:39.820
this isn't really enough data
to have a lot of confidence.

00:36:39.820 --> 00:36:43.794
So we said, well, we still
want to have an answer.

00:36:43.794 --> 00:36:45.460
If I could have the
demo laptop, please.

00:36:48.700 --> 00:36:51.790
All right, so it should
finish in about 10 seconds.

00:36:51.790 --> 00:36:55.450
We'll look at the configuration
of this while we wait.

00:36:55.450 --> 00:36:59.470
So this is a pretty typical
pipeline with a canary release.

00:36:59.470 --> 00:37:01.150
What we encourage
for best practices

00:37:01.150 --> 00:37:02.830
when you're configuring
your own canary

00:37:02.830 --> 00:37:05.230
is don't provision the
new canary resources

00:37:05.230 --> 00:37:07.024
and compare it to
everything else that's

00:37:07.024 --> 00:37:08.440
already been running
in production

00:37:08.440 --> 00:37:09.920
for some period of time.

00:37:09.920 --> 00:37:12.100
Provision a new set
of resources using

00:37:12.100 --> 00:37:16.060
what's running in production
as a baseline and a sibling

00:37:16.060 --> 00:37:17.830
using the canary configuration.

00:37:17.830 --> 00:37:20.140
So it's a very apples
to apples comparison.

00:37:20.140 --> 00:37:22.030
Use the same infrastructure,
provision them

00:37:22.030 --> 00:37:23.440
at the same point in time.

00:37:23.440 --> 00:37:25.270
And the single only
delta should be

00:37:25.270 --> 00:37:26.890
the change you want to canary.

00:37:26.890 --> 00:37:29.630
In this case, it's
a new binary image.

00:37:29.630 --> 00:37:32.200
So that's why you see here
there are a couple of lookups.

00:37:32.200 --> 00:37:34.420
So find the baseline image,
find the canary image.

00:37:34.420 --> 00:37:36.580
And we're just using
tag-based lookups to say,

00:37:36.580 --> 00:37:38.260
what's running in
production, what's

00:37:38.260 --> 00:37:39.843
the new image that's
available, what's

00:37:39.843 --> 00:37:42.100
the change we want to canary?

00:37:42.100 --> 00:37:46.769
Then we deploy a new baseline
and a new canary in parallel.

00:37:46.769 --> 00:37:48.560
And then we have the
canary analysis stage.

00:37:48.560 --> 00:37:50.851
And the way Spinnaker
works, each of these stages

00:37:50.851 --> 00:37:52.100
completes before the next one.

00:37:52.100 --> 00:37:53.761
It's quite standard
control flow.

00:37:53.761 --> 00:37:55.760
You can see the control
flow joins back together

00:37:55.760 --> 00:37:57.171
at the canary analysis stage.

00:37:57.171 --> 00:37:58.670
The only thing worth
keeping in mind

00:37:58.670 --> 00:38:00.652
here is you have
these two deploys.

00:38:00.652 --> 00:38:01.610
These are provisioning.

00:38:01.610 --> 00:38:03.230
Here we're running on GCE VMs.

00:38:03.230 --> 00:38:06.240
It works the same on whatever
platform, but in this case,

00:38:06.240 --> 00:38:09.380
these are GCE VMs running
in managed instance groups.

00:38:09.380 --> 00:38:11.270
Those stages won't be
considered complete

00:38:11.270 --> 00:38:13.310
until they're
considered healthy based

00:38:13.310 --> 00:38:15.950
on a number of things-- load
balancers, machine health,

00:38:15.950 --> 00:38:17.940
and so on.

00:38:17.940 --> 00:38:20.240
So the canary analysis
is quite straightforward.

00:38:20.240 --> 00:38:22.160
In this case, it's a
real time analysis,

00:38:22.160 --> 00:38:24.410
which means we're waiting
for metrics to be published,

00:38:24.410 --> 00:38:25.190
collected.

00:38:25.190 --> 00:38:27.500
You can do a retrospective
analysis as well.

00:38:27.500 --> 00:38:29.330
We refer to a config
and think of that

00:38:29.330 --> 00:38:31.550
as like a templated query.

00:38:31.550 --> 00:38:32.910
That's also done through the UI.

00:38:32.910 --> 00:38:34.701
I don't think we're
going to show that now.

00:38:34.701 --> 00:38:36.350
And then you can see
a warm-up period.

00:38:36.350 --> 00:38:37.790
And you can
configure a lifetime.

00:38:37.790 --> 00:38:41.750
And you can break it down into
multiple intervals and so on.

00:38:41.750 --> 00:38:44.270
And below that, it says,
what's the pair of things

00:38:44.270 --> 00:38:45.230
I want to compare?

00:38:45.230 --> 00:38:46.604
And in this case,
we're comparing

00:38:46.604 --> 00:38:48.260
a baseline and a
canary server group

00:38:48.260 --> 00:38:49.475
that were just provisioned--

00:38:49.475 --> 00:38:50.600
I'll make a little bigger--

00:38:50.600 --> 00:38:52.610
that we're just
provisioned upstream.

00:38:52.610 --> 00:38:56.010
And then there's some additional
configuration for the query.

00:38:56.010 --> 00:38:57.080
So when this thing runs--

00:39:00.260 --> 00:39:02.250
the canary gods smiled upon us.

00:39:02.250 --> 00:39:04.700
So this failed, which
is what we wanted.

00:39:04.700 --> 00:39:06.075
You can see it
says, canary score

00:39:06.075 --> 00:39:08.695
is not above the
marginal score threshold.

00:39:08.695 --> 00:39:10.820
And it does-- you can see
this little red triangle.

00:39:10.820 --> 00:39:12.514
It's admonishing us
that we probably,

00:39:12.514 --> 00:39:14.430
if we're going to rely
on this for production,

00:39:14.430 --> 00:39:16.977
want to have more
data points than that.

00:39:16.977 --> 00:39:19.560
We'll see in a moment, but I'm
sure we have three or four data

00:39:19.560 --> 00:39:20.440
points.

00:39:20.440 --> 00:39:23.350
So this is-- back
out little bit.

00:39:23.350 --> 00:39:25.672
This is what a standard
set of execution details

00:39:25.672 --> 00:39:27.130
look like for a
Spinnaker pipeline.

00:39:27.130 --> 00:39:29.310
In this case, it's
that canary stage.

00:39:29.310 --> 00:39:31.620
But you can see
the configuration,

00:39:31.620 --> 00:39:34.020
which said, what
are we comparing,

00:39:34.020 --> 00:39:37.530
what's the warm up period,
how long does the canary run?

00:39:37.530 --> 00:39:39.490
And then the individual--

00:39:39.490 --> 00:39:41.160
the statuses of the
individual tasks

00:39:41.160 --> 00:39:43.290
that make up that canary stage.

00:39:43.290 --> 00:39:45.612
And this is, I think,
a pretty decent example

00:39:45.612 --> 00:39:47.070
of what you would
have to do if you

00:39:47.070 --> 00:39:48.725
wanted to script this by hand.

00:39:48.725 --> 00:39:50.100
You have to do
this provisioning.

00:39:50.100 --> 00:39:51.766
You have to configure
this thing to wait

00:39:51.766 --> 00:39:52.770
for some period of time.

00:39:52.770 --> 00:39:54.960
You have to make sure
things are healthy.

00:39:54.960 --> 00:39:57.480
Then you have to run the
actual analysis, which

00:39:57.480 --> 00:40:00.300
means go off, build
queries, fetch the metrics,

00:40:00.300 --> 00:40:01.672
figure out how to compare them.

00:40:01.672 --> 00:40:03.630
You can probably find
off-the-shelf statistical

00:40:03.630 --> 00:40:04.500
tests.

00:40:04.500 --> 00:40:06.600
And you could probably
make all of that work.

00:40:06.600 --> 00:40:09.540
But the idea with Spinnaker is
to make all of that consumable.

00:40:09.540 --> 00:40:11.000
I mean, we're trying
to kind of let

00:40:11.000 --> 00:40:12.750
you work with a higher
level of primitive.

00:40:12.750 --> 00:40:16.750
And the canary analysis is
the latest example of that.

00:40:16.750 --> 00:40:17.620
So this completed.

00:40:17.620 --> 00:40:21.820
If we look at the results,
we can drill down.

00:40:21.820 --> 00:40:23.680
And now this is one metric.

00:40:23.680 --> 00:40:24.590
It's very simple.

00:40:24.590 --> 00:40:26.880
So the graph is
also quite simple.

00:40:26.880 --> 00:40:29.980
But you can see on the left,
the name of that metric.

00:40:29.980 --> 00:40:32.410
You can see that the
classification logic determined

00:40:32.410 --> 00:40:34.990
it to be high, so the
canary was high with respect

00:40:34.990 --> 00:40:36.544
to the baseline.

00:40:36.544 --> 00:40:38.960
In this case, the only score
we possibly could have gotten

00:40:38.960 --> 00:40:41.190
is 0 or 100, because we
only have the one metric,

00:40:41.190 --> 00:40:44.291
so you can't use any
weighted scoring or anything.

00:40:44.291 --> 00:40:46.040
And then you can see
the two lines across.

00:40:46.040 --> 00:40:48.320
Hopefully, you can see the
two lines across the graph.

00:40:48.320 --> 00:40:50.300
The canary is blue
and the baseline

00:40:50.300 --> 00:40:52.490
is maybe golden axelrod.

00:40:52.490 --> 00:40:53.900
I don't know what color that is.

00:40:53.900 --> 00:40:55.470
But you have the
two lines there.

00:40:55.470 --> 00:40:56.250
And then at the
bottom, there are

00:40:56.250 --> 00:40:57.660
some simple statistical details.

00:40:57.660 --> 00:41:00.860
And you can see it said it got
three data points for each.

00:41:00.860 --> 00:41:03.947
And the nice thing about
the way we surface this

00:41:03.947 --> 00:41:05.280
is that everything is available.

00:41:05.280 --> 00:41:06.910
So you can actually drill
down right from the UI.

00:41:06.910 --> 00:41:08.520
And you can see the
resulting queries

00:41:08.520 --> 00:41:10.680
that were expanded and
executed, in this case,

00:41:10.680 --> 00:41:11.610
against Stackdriver.

00:41:11.610 --> 00:41:14.127
But it works the same no
matter the underlying system.

00:41:14.127 --> 00:41:15.210
So you can see the metric.

00:41:15.210 --> 00:41:16.420
You can see the queries.

00:41:16.420 --> 00:41:18.240
And you can see the
actual data points that

00:41:18.240 --> 00:41:21.792
were returned from Stackdriver.

00:41:21.792 --> 00:41:24.950
All right, if I could have
the slides laptop again.

00:41:35.930 --> 00:41:38.180
Don't need the video.

00:41:38.180 --> 00:41:40.420
All right, so what's next?

00:41:40.420 --> 00:41:43.450
I think we've given a
pretty good example of where

00:41:43.450 --> 00:41:44.160
we are now.

00:41:44.160 --> 00:41:45.970
And we wanted to
talk about what's

00:41:45.970 --> 00:41:47.940
coming in the coming months.

00:41:47.940 --> 00:41:50.440
The Spinnaker community itself
is pretty warm and welcoming.

00:41:50.440 --> 00:41:52.148
And like I said, tons
of folks are always

00:41:52.148 --> 00:41:54.610
in the Slack channel,
always willing to help.

00:41:54.610 --> 00:41:58.360
But the code base itself is
still kind of like a tough nut

00:41:58.360 --> 00:41:58.870
to crack.

00:41:58.870 --> 00:42:01.453
So we want to figure out how to
make it easier for other folks

00:42:01.453 --> 00:42:02.320
to get involved.

00:42:02.320 --> 00:42:04.630
We've lately been writing
more developer guides

00:42:04.630 --> 00:42:05.800
and we're hosting workshops.

00:42:05.800 --> 00:42:09.250
And this is we, the whole
community, not just Google.

00:42:09.250 --> 00:42:10.690
But this is one
of the main things

00:42:10.690 --> 00:42:12.170
we're focused on at the moment.

00:42:12.170 --> 00:42:15.680
Also, to really formalize some
of our processes and procedures

00:42:15.680 --> 00:42:17.680
in terms of how the
Spinnaker community operates

00:42:17.680 --> 00:42:19.850
and how people can
become committers

00:42:19.850 --> 00:42:21.610
and those kinds of things.

00:42:21.610 --> 00:42:24.880
The set of cloud platforms
is always continuing to grow.

00:42:24.880 --> 00:42:26.530
There's support for
this thing called

00:42:26.530 --> 00:42:31.060
artifacts, which is the idea
of instead of embedding logic

00:42:31.060 --> 00:42:32.740
deep within Spinnaker
to understand

00:42:32.740 --> 00:42:36.250
what comes from Jenkins, or
what comes from a container

00:42:36.250 --> 00:42:38.230
registry, externalize
that information.

00:42:38.230 --> 00:42:40.780
So an artifact would
say, this type of thing

00:42:40.780 --> 00:42:43.510
from this location with this
address and this metadata.

00:42:43.510 --> 00:42:44.849
So we call that artifacts.

00:42:44.849 --> 00:42:46.390
But we're making
that more consumable

00:42:46.390 --> 00:42:48.670
and adding support for
additional systems.

00:42:48.670 --> 00:42:53.080
The canary stage that we showed
had upstream deployment stages,

00:42:53.080 --> 00:42:54.550
and then later on,
those things you

00:42:54.550 --> 00:42:57.255
provisioned to do the comparison
have to be cleaned up.

00:42:57.255 --> 00:42:59.380
We're adding support directly
into the canary stage

00:42:59.380 --> 00:43:01.810
for doing that orchestration
to make it easier

00:43:01.810 --> 00:43:04.120
to configure canary releases.

00:43:04.120 --> 00:43:07.270
There's a CLI which, at present,
lets you programmatically

00:43:07.270 --> 00:43:09.380
publish and interact
with pipelines.

00:43:09.380 --> 00:43:12.290
We're broadening the set
of capabilities of the CLI.

00:43:12.290 --> 00:43:14.830
There's work underway
inside of Netflix

00:43:14.830 --> 00:43:17.980
for the time being to add
support for a SQL backend

00:43:17.980 --> 00:43:21.100
as an alternative for in-flight
workflow representation,

00:43:21.100 --> 00:43:24.790
provide more durability and
some other capabilities.

00:43:24.790 --> 00:43:28.120
And there are several streams
of work underway involving

00:43:28.120 --> 00:43:31.770
configuration as code, from
manifest oriented deployments

00:43:31.770 --> 00:43:34.380
to approaches where you
declare the desired state,

00:43:34.380 --> 00:43:36.130
and then it's constantly
trying to achieve

00:43:36.130 --> 00:43:39.490
that state on some schedule, and
a bunch of other related bits.

00:43:39.490 --> 00:43:42.550
And just think of it
as a spectrum from I

00:43:42.550 --> 00:43:45.160
do everything with a UI and
a wizard and fill in fields,

00:43:45.160 --> 00:43:47.410
or I check in my
manifest somewhere.

00:43:47.410 --> 00:43:50.150
And then whether it's a
source control system,

00:43:50.150 --> 00:43:52.300
or some system that
supports buckets.

00:43:52.300 --> 00:43:56.450
And then Spinnaker tries
to achieve that state.

00:43:56.450 --> 00:43:58.940
All right, best is yet to come.

00:43:58.940 --> 00:44:00.802
In terms of getting
involved, we have a site

00:44:00.802 --> 00:44:02.010
that has tons of information.

00:44:02.010 --> 00:44:03.634
There are code labs,
tutorials, there's

00:44:03.634 --> 00:44:05.310
documentation,
reference architectures.

00:44:05.310 --> 00:44:07.400
There's all kinds of
information up there.

00:44:07.400 --> 00:44:10.220
We have the Slack channel where
there's-- there are people

00:44:10.220 --> 00:44:13.580
in there 24 hours a day, always
willing to answer questions.

00:44:13.580 --> 00:44:15.756
There's a discourse
community forum.

00:44:15.756 --> 00:44:17.630
There are I don't know
how many GitHub repos,

00:44:17.630 --> 00:44:19.354
but that's the umbrella repo.

00:44:19.354 --> 00:44:20.270
That's where to start.

00:44:20.270 --> 00:44:22.103
That's where most of
the issues are tracked,

00:44:22.103 --> 00:44:23.990
so that's a good starting point.

00:44:23.990 --> 00:44:25.370
And I don't want
to forget, we're

00:44:25.370 --> 00:44:28.610
having a summit in
October in Seattle.

00:44:28.610 --> 00:44:30.320
Looks like it's
going to be huge.

00:44:30.320 --> 00:44:31.410
Come join us.

00:44:31.410 --> 00:44:32.100
It's open.

00:44:32.100 --> 00:44:34.100
It's not just for folks
who are already involved

00:44:34.100 --> 00:44:35.070
in the Spinnaker community.

00:44:35.070 --> 00:44:36.778
It's for people who
want to get involved,

00:44:36.778 --> 00:44:39.800
whether as developers, or users,
or partners, or anything else.

00:44:39.800 --> 00:44:41.750
So please come join us.

00:44:41.750 --> 00:44:45.100
[MUSIC PLAYING]

